clean - takes metadata out of files
===================================

SYNOPSIS

    clean [-bHnpqv] FILE...          clean each file, in place
    clean [-Hnpqv] -o OUT FILE       a clean copy of FILE in OUT
    clean [-Hnqv] - < IN > OUT       standard input to standard output
    clean -u [-nqv] FILE...          undo: the newest backup back
    clean -h                         help

DESCRIPTION

Pictures, documents, video and audio carry more than you see: the camera
and its serial number, where a photo was taken, who wrote a document
and with which program, dates, earlier versions, comments. clean takes
it out, in place: the file is replaced by its clean version, and the
original is kept in ~/.system/clean first.

It says what it took out:

    photo.jpg: removed EXIF (camera, date, GPS position), thumbnail;
        new random bytes (a hash of its own)
    report.pdf: removed document information (Author, Creator,
        Producer, CreationDate), XMP, file ID, earlier versions and
        unused parts (12)

A file it doesn't know, or one that is broken, is left as it was and
named on stderr, never passed off as clean. Running it again on a
clean file says "nothing to remove".

OPTIONS

    -o OUT  write a clean copy of FILE to OUT and leave FILE as it was
            (no backup needed). OUT must not exist yet: clean never
            writes over a file. With - as FILE the copy comes from
            standard input; -o - (or just "clean -") writes it to
            standard output, and the messages go to stderr.
    -b      no backup (then -u can't undo it)
    -H      same hash: no new random bytes (see "A hash of its own");
            only what goes changes the file
    -p      keep the file's times (else 2000-01-01 00:00 UTC)
    -n      dry run: say what would go, change nothing (with -u: say
            which backup would come back)
    -u      undo: put the newest backup of each FILE back. The backup
            then goes; -u again puts the one before it back, and so on
            back to the original.
    -q      say nothing but errors
    -v      also say where each backup went
    -h      help (also --help)
    --      end of options: a FILE named -x is clean -- -x

Options go before the files; a FILE starting with - needs --.

EXIT STATUS

    0   every file was cleaned (or had nothing to remove)
    1   a file was not: it is named on stderr, with why, and left as
        it was (the other files are still cleaned)
    2   wrong options; nothing was done

EXAMPLES

    clean photo.jpg report.pdf          clean both, in place
    clean -n photo.jpg                  only look
    clean -o share.jpg photo.jpg        a clean copy to share
    clean - < photo.jpg > share.jpg     the same, as a filter
    clean -u photo.jpg                  oops: the original back
    find ~/pictures -type f -exec clean -q -H {} +
                                        a whole directory (-H: see
                                        "A hash of its own")

From standard input clean works on a copy in RAM ($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR or
/dev/shm), so the metadata doesn't reach the disk; video and audio
must fit there.

CHANGED OPTIONS

So that a letter means the same in clean and seal: -k (keep the times)
is now -p, and -s (same hash) is now -H. The old letters stop with a
message saying the new one. New: -o, - (standard input), -v, -h, and
-u going further back each time.


What goes
---------

JPEG       EXIF (camera, serial number, GPS, dates, program, maker
           notes), its thumbnail (it can show the photo before it was
           cropped), XMP, IPTC/Photoshop, comments, program data, more
           pictures (MPF) and anything after the picture. The rotation
           stays (a tiny EXIF with only that), so photos don't turn
           sideways. The colour profile stays, so colours don't change.
PNG        text (author, program, comments), date, EXIF, pixel size,
           program chunks, anything after the picture
GIF        comments, XMP, program data (the loop setting stays)
WebP       EXIF (rotation kept), XMP
TIFF       camera, program, dates, author, computer name, copyright,
           description, XMP, IPTC/Photoshop (and its layers), GPS,
           EXIF, Windows' tags, rating, unique ID
camera raw DNG, NEF, CR2, ARW, ORF, RW2, PEF (all built on TIFF): the
           same, but what raw programs need to show the picture stays:
           the camera's make and model, the EXIF exposure data and the
           maker notes. Out of EXIF go the dates, serial numbers,
           owner's name, comment and unique ID; out of DNG the camera's
           serial number and the original raw file and its name.
SVG        comments, metadata (author, title, licence), the editor's
           settings, file names and directories Inkscape writes into it
           (where it was exported to), the program's name,
           Illustrator's private copy of the drawing
BMP        a colour profile's file name (a path on the computer)
HEIF, AVIF EXIF (camera, GPS, dates) and XMP, blanked where they are
           (the rotation is not in EXIF in these files)
PDF        document information (author, title, program, dates), XMP
           everywhere, program data (PieceInfo: Illustrator keeps the
           whole original there), dates and authors of comments, the
           file ID, and every earlier version that edits left inside
           the file. A new PDF is written from the parts still used;
           pages, fonts and pictures are copied byte for byte.
MP4, MOV,  user data and tags (GPS position, camera, date, program),
M4A        XMP, the creation dates of the file and each track, the
           encoder's name
MKV, WebM  tags, title, date, muxing and writing program, attachments
           (cover pictures, and fonts for subtitles)
MP3        ID3v2 and ID3v1 tags (titles, pictures, comments, program),
           the encoder's name and version in its tag (it becomes
           "LAME", which players need to find the gaps at each end)
Ogg        tags of Vorbis, Opus and Theora (titles, comments, program)
AAC        the encoder's name (ID3 tags, as in MP3)
FLAC       tags, pictures, program data
WAV, AVI   INFO tags, ID3, recorder data (bext), XMP
AIFF       name, author, copyright, notes, comments, ID3, XMP, program
           data
WMA, WMV   tags (title, author, copyright, description, program,
           encoder settings, more tags), branding, the creation date,
           the file ID (it gets new random bytes: it names this file)
DOCX,      the document information (author, last editor, company,
XLSX, PPTX manager, dates, editing time, revision count, template,
           program, title, keywords, custom properties), the thumbnail
           (it can show an earlier version), names, initials, user IDs
           (e-mail addresses) and dates of comments and tracked
           changes, Word's editing session IDs (rsid: they tie
           documents edited in the same session together), paths on
           the computer (Excel keeps where it was saved, Word where
           its template is)
ODT, ODS,  the same in LibreOffice's files: meta.xml (author, program,
ODP        dates, editing time, template), the thumbnail, names and
           dates of comments and tracked changes, the printer's name
           and its settings
EPUB       the program's tags (calibre, Sigil) and the change date
           (which EPUB 3 needs: it becomes 2000-01-01). The book's own
           author, title and ID stay: they are the book.
tar        file times, owners (user and group names and IDs), and in
           pax headers more times, owner names and extended attributes
           (a browser notes there where a file was downloaded from)
gzip       the original file's name, its time and system, comments;
           in a .tar.gz the tar inside too
zip        and inside all of the above: file times, owner IDs, the
           system it was made on, comments, anything after it. Every
           picture inside is cleaned as a picture. The words stay
           exactly the same; only the parts that changed are packed
           again.
video      the settings text x264, x265 and FFmpeg put into the
           first picture (its bytes become spaces)

Pictures are not decoded and encoded again: they stay exactly the
same. Video and audio can be big, so they are changed where they are:
what goes becomes padding of the same size, so nothing else moves.

The file's times become 2000-01-01 00:00 UTC (-p keeps them).


A hash of its own
-----------------

A file's hash (its SHA-256, say) can follow it: two clean copies of
the same photo would have the same hash, so whoever sees both knows
they are one file, and a hash noted once finds the file again. So each
time clean changes a file it also changes what the format lets vary
without changing the content, at random:

JPEG       0 to 31 fill bytes (0xFF, which decoders skip) before each
           marker
PNG        the picture data split into four chunks at random places
GIF        the picture data in sub-blocks of random sizes
PDF        the four binary bytes on the second line
MP4, MOV,  32 random bytes in a padding box at the end
HEIF, AVIF
Matroska   32 random bytes in a Void element at the end
FLAC       32 random bytes in the padding block
Ogg        32 random bytes in the padding after the tags
WebP       32 random bytes in a chunk of their own at the end (a
           simple WebP gets the extended header that allows one)
MP3        a random "private" bit in the headers of the first 1024
           sound frames (decoders don't read it)
WAV, AVI,  32 random bytes in a JUNK chunk at the end
AIFF
AAC        a random "private" bit in the frame headers, as in MP3
WMA, WMV   a new random file ID
TIFF, raw  32 random bytes at the end, which nothing points to
SVG        32 random spaces, tabs and line ends after the drawing
BMP        32 random bytes after the picture
tar        32 random bytes after the two empty blocks that end it
gzip       a comment of 32 random spaces and tabs
DOCX, ODT, 32 random spaces, tabs and line ends after the document
EPUB...    information (or the book's description); in a plain zip,
           a comment of them

Nothing of it says "clean". It happens on every run, even when nothing
else goes ("nothing to remove; new random bytes"), so each copy you
share gets its own hash; clean -H leaves it out. Running clean over a
whole directory again rewrites every file (and backs each one up),
so use -H there, or -b. The picture and the sound stay exactly the
same: this was checked by decoding each format before and after.

Not the same thing: "perceptual" hashes (PhotoDNA and the like) look at
the picture itself, and these bytes don't change it. Only changing the
picture (cropping, resizing) changes those.


What it leaves
--------------

- Encrypted PDFs, and signed ones (cleaning breaks the signature, and
  the signature itself names the signer).
- Not yet: BigTIFF, zip64 (zip files over 4 GB), Canon's CR3, the
  older office files (DOC, XLS, PPT), and PDFs packed in ways other
  than Flate.
- Encrypted zip files.
- Not yet: 7z (its list of files, with their times, is packed with
  LZMA, which clean can't unpack yet), and the tar inside .tar.xz,
  .tar.bz2 and .tar.zst (xz, bzip2 and zstd themselves hold no names
  or times). RAR files can't be written again by free software.
- Pictures inside a tar keep their metadata: clean them before
  packing (tar is changed in place, and a cleaned picture is smaller).
- In raw files: the maker notes (raw programs need them for the
  colours; some cameras put the serial number in them too), and the
  previews inside, as the camera made them.
- What is written in a document: names in the text, in headers and
  footers, or a title page.
- In MP3, the "LAME3.100" LAME writes into unused bits of the sound
  frames: they turned out not to be unused by every decoder, so
  changing them could change the sound.
- What is in the picture or sound itself: faces, places, a voice,
  what a screenshot shows. A screenshot of the whole screen shows
  dwm's bar, with your time.
- Track names and languages in videos, chapters.


The backups
-----------

Each original goes to ~/.system/clean (only yours, mode 700),
named by the time (UTC); the file "list" there says which file each
one was. The backups still hold all the metadata: delete them when you
no longer need an undo:

    rm -r ~/.system/clean

Or make none: clean -b. A picture or document is then still replaced
in one step (a new file, renamed over the old one), so a crash leaves
either the old file or the clean one. Video and audio are changed
where they are, so a crash or a full disk in the middle can leave one
half done: keep -b for files you have another copy of.


Build and install
-----------------

Plain C (C89), only libc, static. install.sh builds it into
~/.system/bin (no root needed):

    ~/dotfiles/install.sh clean

By hand (the build makes clean-bin, since "make clean" removes it):

    make
    make test          sample files (made with ImageMagick and ffmpeg;
                       formats without their tool are skipped), the
                       command line, and 200 broken copies of each
                       sample (FUZZ=n make test for more or fewer)
    make PREFIX=$HOME/.system install

Files: clean.c (the command, backups), img.c (JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP),
media.c (video, audio, HEIF), ogg.c (Ogg), more.c (AIFF, WMA/WMV,
TIFF, raw), svg.c (SVG, BMP), zip.c (zip, office documents, EPUB),
archive.c (tar, gzip), test/run.sh (the tests), xml.c (edits XML parts), deflate.c (packs the
changed parts of zip and gzip files), pdf.c (PDF), rand.c (the random
bytes), inflate.c (unpacks the compressed parts of PDFs, zip and gzip
files; it stops at a size limit,
so a zip bomb can't fill the memory).
